# [IDR](../wg/idr.html)

## Summary

The Inter-Domain Routing (IDR) Working Group held an interim meeting to review ongoing work across Segment Routing (SR) and Traffic Engineering (TE) extensions, BGP Flow Specification Version 2 (FSv2) specifications, BGP base protocol maintenance (`draft-ietf-idr-bgp4-rfc4271bis`), BGP best path next-hop selection mechanisms, and core protocol adoption candidates. Key presentations included updates on SR Policy BFD parameters, Network Resource Partitioning (NRP), FSv2 filter and action components, `draft-ietf-idr-bgp4-rfc4271bis` error handling integration, BGP next-hop selection framework, Add-Path limits, and Only-To-Customer (OTC) route server verification.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### 1. SR and TE Drafts

* **BGP SR Policy Indications for BFD Configuration**
  * **Presentation:** Presented by liusong ([02-SR Policy BFD extension](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-idr-03/materials/slides-interim-2026-idr-03-sessa-02-sr-policy-bfd-extension-00)).
  * **Discussion:** The draft defines optional S-BFD parameters (Desired Min TX Interval, Detection Multiplier) within the encapsulation attribute carried in SR Policy NLRIs to unify policy and monitoring intent. Sue Hares and Jeffrey Haas noted that while BFD usage with SR is common, carrying BFD provisioning parameters in BGP state is novel; input will be coordinated with the BFD and SPRING working groups.

* **BGP Extensions for Network Resource Partition (NRP)**
  * **Presentation:** Presented by ruiqianhu ([03 -draft-li-idr-bgp-nrp NRP Ext. Community](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-idr-03/materials/slides-interim-2026-idr-03-sessa-03-draft-li-idr-bgp-nrp-nrp-ext-community-00); context in `draft-ietf-idr-bgp-ls-sr-policy-nrp`).
  * **Discussion:** Proposes decoupling SR policies from NRPs by advertising NRP IDs in standard routing updates via a new opaque extended community, allowing multiple NRPs to share a common SR policy. Keyur Patel and Jeffrey Haas raised concerns regarding BGP PDU space consumption (8 octets per extended community) and potential issues if extended communities are filtered or dropped along the path.

